  "$ROOT/scripts/check-gate-image" --root "$ROOT"
}

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-repository verify lock (#3315)
#
# Two concurrent `scripts/test verify` invocations on one worktree family contend
# on golangci-lint's global lock and on the shared testcontainers Postgres, and
# the loser fails category-B behind a misleading scope-drift framing (#2645). The
# lock makes that structurally impossible on the normal path.
#
# KEY: <git-common-dir>/fishhawk-verify.lock, or $FISHHAWK_VERIFY_LOCK_PATH when
# set. The common dir is SHARED by the main checkout and every linked worktree,
# so an agent's run worktree and a shell verify in the main checkout genuinely
# contend — which a TMPDIR-keyed path would not guarantee, since the runner's
# gate env is a default-deny allow-list and a differing TMPDIR would leave the
# control inert while appearing to work. The runner's throwaway verify checkout
# is a CLONE with a common dir of its own (ADR-063 / #2134), so the runner
# injects the PRIMARY's lock path via FISHHAWK_VERIFY_LOCK_PATH; see
# _verify_lock_path. Living inside .git also keeps it invisible to `git
# status`, so it can never pollute the scope-completeness or untracked-file
# gates.
#
# PRIMITIVE: an atomic symlink whose TARGET is `<pid>:<kind>` (kind is `runner`
# or `shell`). `ln -s` is one symlink(2) that fails EEXIST if the path is taken,
# so the lock carries BOTH its owner and its kind from the instant it exists —
# there is no window in which it exists yet is unowned. Same construction as
# _lease_lock (#1792).
#
# SEMANTICS, by the kind of the invocation MEETING a live holder:
#   * SHELL meets ANY live holder  -> REFUSE IMMEDIATELY. Non-zero, no sleep, no
#     poll, and an actionable message naming `scripts/test single`. Polling a
#     refusal in a loop is what killed run a662ed6f category-A; the refusal is a
#     fast failure, not a queue.
#   * RUNNER meets a live SHELL holder -> WAIT, bounded by 600 seconds at a
#     2-second poll. Most holders finish inside that, and the runner then
#     acquires normally with NO concurrency at all. Only on EXPIRY does it
#     DISPLACE the holder, printing a loud warning naming the deposed pid. That
#     is the wedged-holder escape hatch, not the normal path.
#   * RUNNER meets a live RUNNER holder -> wait on the same budget and then
#     REFUSE. Two concurrent runner verifies on one worktree family is a bug to
#     surface, not one to paper over by stealing.
#
# DEAD/GARBAGE holders are pruned, and the prune is itself SERIALISED by a second
# atomic symlink at <lockpath>.prune. Without that, two invocations that both
# classify the same dead holder can both unlink it, and the loser's unlink
# removes the WINNER'S fresh live lock — both then believe they hold it.
# Re-reading immediately before the unlink only narrows that window; the marker
# closes it. Holding the marker the pruner RE-READS the lock and only removes a
# still-dead holder; if a live holder appeared it falls through to the normal
# live-holder path. The marker is released by the same EXIT trap, only when this
# invocation created it.
#
# The marker's OWN dead-owner recovery is exclusive too (_verify_lock_recover_
# marker): a claim at <lockpath>.prune.reap.<victim>, content-addressed by the
# observed dead owner, and a removal CONDITIONAL on the marker still naming that
# owner. An unprotected read/kill/rm there would be the identical defect one
# level up — two recoverers, the loser deleting the winner's fresh live marker,
# both then pruning the lock. A claim is never broken, so a recoverer killed
# while holding one wedges recovery of that one marker; the restart budget turns
# that into a loud refusal naming the paths to remove, which is a residual an
# operator can clear rather than a mutual exclusion that is merely narrow.
#
# The runner's EXPIRY displacement is conditional in the same sense
# (_verify_lock_steal): the lock is taken by an atomic `mv` to a private path and
# the entry actually taken is READ. Anything other than the holder the budget was
# spent on is put back and the displacement refused, so the decision stays valid
# THROUGH the unlink rather than only at classification time — a shell holder
# that releases into a fresh RUNNER holder in that window is met by the normal
# live-runner refusal instead of being deleted.
#
# RESIDUAL, stated: `kill -0 <pid>` succeeding does not prove the pid is the
# ORIGINAL holder — a reused pid reads as live. That is the identical residual
# the container lease already accepts, and the consequence here is a bounded wait
# plus a loud displacement, or a refusal with an escape hatch printed in the
# message — never a lost or corrupted run.
#
# The lock is taken by `verify` ONLY, so `test`/`coverage`/`lint`/`single` are
# unaffected and the escape hatch the refusal names stays always available.
# Long form: scripts/README.md.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Wait budget and poll interval, by number. Both are dev-overridable via the
# environment; that override is DEV-ONLY in the same sense as the other FISHHAWK_*
# knobs, since the runner's gate env is a default-deny allow-list and FISHHAWK_ is
# not an allowed prefix. Read at USE time (not frozen at load) so a per-invocation
# override is honoured even when this file is sourced lib-only by the harness.
VERIFY_LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS=600
VERIFY_LOCK_POLL_SECONDS=2

_verify_lock_wait_budget() {
  local v="${FISHHAWK_VERIFY_LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS:-$VERIFY_LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS}"
  case "$v" in ''|*[!0-9]*) v="$VERIFY_LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS" ;; esac
  printf '%s' "$v"
}

_verify_lock_poll_interval() {
  local v="${FISHHAWK_VERIFY_LOCK_POLL_SECONDS:-$VERIFY_LOCK_POLL_SECONDS}"
  case "$v" in ''|*[!0-9]*|0) v="$VERIFY_LOCK_POLL_SECONDS" ;; esac
  printf '%s' "$v"
}

# _verify_lock_self_pid: this invocation's identity. Injectable (as the container
# lease's _lease_token is) so the harness can drive two synthetic acquisitions in
# one process and pin the prune interleaving deterministically.
_verify_lock_self_pid() {
  printf '%s' "${FISHHAWK_VERIFY_LOCK_SELF_PID:-$$}"
}

# _verify_lock_kind: `runner` when the runner marked this invocation, else
# `shell`. The runner appends the marker to the gate env AFTER sanitization, so
# an ambient value never reaches the gate subprocess.
_verify_lock_kind() {
  if [ "${FISHHAWK_VERIFY_LOCK_OWNER:-}" = "runner" ]; then
    printf 'runner'
  else
    printf 'shell'
  fi
}

# _verify_lock_path: $FISHHAWK_VERIFY_LOCK_PATH when set, else
# <git-common-dir>/fishhawk-verify.lock; or non-zero plus a printed reason when
# locking is not possible.
#
# The OVERRIDE is consulted FIRST (ADR-063 / #2134). The runner's throwaway
# verify checkout is no longer a linked worktree sharing this repository's
# common dir — it is a `git clone --no-hardlinks` with a common dir of its OWN,
# so the common-dir derivation below would key the runner's lock to a directory
# nothing else ever looks at, and the #2645 contention (a runner verify and an
# agent-shell verify racing on golangci-lint's global lock and the shared
# testcontainers Postgres) would silently reopen. The runner therefore resolves
# the PRIMARY repository's `<git-common-dir>/fishhawk-verify.lock` and injects it
# as FISHHAWK_VERIFY_LOCK_PATH after sanitization — the same post-sanitization
# injection FISHHAWK_VERIFY_LOCK_OWNER rides (the gate env is a default-deny
# allow-list, so an ambient value never reaches the gate subprocess; the name is
# additionally on the runner's and the CLI's explicit deny-lists). An override
# whose PARENT directory does not exist degrades to NO locking with a one-line
# reason naming the variable, never to a lock silently keyed elsewhere.
#
# Unset, the derivation is unchanged. Three DEGRADES, each to NO locking rather
# than to a failure: git absent, $ROOT not a work tree, and a BARE repo. The
# bare-repo case guards on the printed `--is-inside-work-tree` VALUE, not its
# exit status, because a bare repo prints `false` yet exits 0 — the same trap
# _verify_schema_sync documents.
_verify_lock_path() {
  if [ -n "${FISHHAWK_VERIFY_LOCK_PATH:-}" ]; then
    local override="$FISHHAWK_VERIFY_LOCK_PATH" parent
    parent="$(dirname "$override")"
    if [ ! -d "$parent" ]; then
      echo "scripts/test: FISHHAWK_VERIFY_LOCK_PATH=$override names a lock whose parent directory does not exist — running verify without the concurrency lock" >&2
      return 1
    fi
    printf '%s' "$override"
    return 0
  fi
  if ! command -v git >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "scripts/test: git not found — running verify without the concurrency lock" >&2
    return 1
  fi
  if [ "$(git -C "$ROOT" rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree 2>/dev/null)" != "true" ]; then
    echo "scripts/test: $ROOT is not a git work tree — running verify without the concurrency lock" >&2
    return 1
  fi
  local common
  if ! common="$(git -C "$ROOT" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null)" || [ -z "$common" ]; then
    echo "scripts/test: could not resolve the git common dir — running verify without the concurrency lock" >&2
    return 1
  fi
  printf '%s/fishhawk-verify.lock' "${common%/}"
}

# _verify_lock_classify <lockpath>: ONE parse driving every branch below, so the
# three callers cannot diverge. Prints exactly one of:
#   free | dead | live-shell | live-runner | garbage
_verify_lock_classify() {
  local lock="$1" target pid kind
  if [ ! -L "$lock" ]; then
    if [ -e "$lock" ]; then printf 'garbage'; else printf 'free'; fi
    return 0
  fi
  target="$(readlink "$lock" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  pid="${target%%:*}"
  kind="${target#*:}"
  case "$pid" in ''|*[!0-9]*) printf 'garbage'; return 0 ;; esac
  case "$kind" in runner|shell) ;; *) printf 'garbage'; return 0 ;; esac
  if kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null; then
    printf 'live-%s' "$kind"
  else
    printf 'dead'
  fi
}

# _verify_lock_holder_pid <lockpath>: the holder pid, or empty.
_verify_lock_holder_pid() {
  local target
  target="$(readlink "$1" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  case "${target%%:*}" in
    ''|*[!0-9]*) printf '' ;;
    *) printf '%s' "${target%%:*}" ;;
  esac
}

# _verify_lock_try_acquire <lockpath> <target>: the atomic claim. The
# readlink-equals-ours re-check rejects the case where `ln -s` "succeeded" by
# landing a stray link INSIDE a leftover dir-form lock, so only a genuine atomic
# claim returns success.
_verify_lock_try_acquire() {
  local lock="$1" target="$2"
  ln -s "$target" "$lock" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  [ "$(readlink "$lock" 2>/dev/null)" = "$target" ] || return 1
  VERIFY_LOCK_HELD="$lock"
  VERIFY_LOCK_TARGET="$target"
  trap "$EXIT_TRAP" EXIT
  return 0
}

# _verify_lock_recover_marker <markerpath> <observed-owner>: recover a prune
# marker left behind by a pruner that died holding it — WITHOUT the unlink race
# the marker itself exists to close.
#
# The naive form (read the owner, `kill -0` it, `rm`) is the SAME defect one
# level up: two recoverers can both observe the same dead owner, A removes the
# marker and plants its own, and B — still acting on the classification it took
# BEFORE A ran — deletes A's FRESH LIVE marker. Both then believe they are the
# sole pruner and both prune the lock, which is exactly the double-prune this
# marker was introduced to prevent.
#
# The observed owner is therefore an ARGUMENT, not something re-read here: the
# staleness is the hazard, so it is modelled explicitly and the removal is made
# both EXCLUSIVE and CONDITIONAL.
#
#   * The right to reap a marker owned by <victim> is claimed by an atomic
#     symlink at <marker>.reap.<victim>. The claim is CONTENT-ADDRESSED by the
#     victim, so two recoverers acting on the same observation cannot both hold
#     it, and one acting on a DIFFERENT observation is not competing for the
#     same removal at all.
#   * Holding the claim, the marker is RE-READ and removed ONLY if it still
#     names <victim>. A fresh marker planted by whoever got there first names a
#     different pid and is left strictly alone.
#
# Nothing is created at <marker> here. The caller restarts classification and
# competes for the marker with the same atomic `ln -s` every other invocation
# uses, so a successful recovery confers no ownership. It returns 0 ONLY when it
# actually removed the marker it was asked about, and 1 for every back-off — a
# live owner, a claim held by another recoverer, or a marker that has since
# changed hands.
#
# This function NEVER breaks a claim it finds, not even one whose owner is dead:
# breaking a claim would reintroduce the identical race one level deeper, and
# each further level could only ever narrow the window rather than close it. The
# terminating alternative is stated honestly instead. A claim leaked by a
# recoverer SIGKILLed inside the microseconds it holds one wedges recovery of
# that ONE dead marker; the acquisition loop's restart budget converts that into
# a loud refusal naming the paths to remove. A wedge an operator can clear in
# one `rm` beats a mutual exclusion that is merely narrow.
_verify_lock_recover_marker() {
  local marker="$1" victim="$2" claim self cur rc
  self="$(_verify_lock_self_pid)"
  case "$victim" in
    ''|*[!0-9]*)
      # No pid to identify a live pruner with: a GARBAGE marker (a dir-form
      # leftover, or a target that names no pid). It still goes through an
      # exclusive claim, so two recoverers cannot both act on it.
      claim="$marker.reap.garbage"
      ;;
    *)
      if kill -0 "$victim" 2>/dev/null; then
        return 1  # a LIVE pruner holds it: there is nothing to recover
      fi
      claim="$marker.reap.$victim"
      ;;
  esac
  if ! { ln -s "$self" "$claim" 2>/dev/null && [ "$(readlink "$claim" 2>/dev/null)" = "$self" ]; }; then
    return 1
  fi
  cur="$(readlink "$marker" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  rc=1
  if [ "$cur" = "$victim" ]; then
    rm -rf "$marker" 2>/dev/null || true
    rc=0
  fi
  rm -f "$claim" 2>/dev/null || true
  return "$rc"
}

# _verify_lock_steal <lockpath> <victim-target> <target>: the runner's
# wait-expiry displacement, made valid THROUGH the removal rather than only at
# classification time.
#
# The unconditional `rm -f "$lock"` this replaces decided on a classification
# and acted on it afterwards. Between the two, the wedged shell holder can
# release and a DIFFERENT invocation — including a RUNNER one — can acquire; the
# rm would then delete a fresh lock this loop never classified, producing exactly
# the two concurrent runner verifies the live-runner branch exists to refuse.
#
# `rm` cannot be made conditional, but `mv` can be made EXCLUSIVE: renaming the
# lock to a private path is atomic, so of two invocations racing to displace the
# same entry exactly one succeeds — and the winner can then READ what it actually
# took. Taking anything other than the holder the budget was spent on is a
# MIS-TAKE: the entry is put back with a non-clobbering `ln -s` and this
# invocation REFUSES, so the caller restarts classification and meets the new
# holder through the normal branches. A displacement is only ever completed
# against the holder that was actually waited on.
#
# The prune marker is held across the whole sequence, so a concurrent PRUNER
# (which also removes the lock) is excluded while the rename excludes a
# concurrent STEALER.
#
# RESIDUAL, stated rather than claimed closed: the restore is `ln -s`, which
# refuses to clobber. If a THIRD invocation acquired the freed path between the
# mis-take and the restore, the mis-taken holder's entry is gone and it becomes a
# phantom holder. That needs two separate acquisitions inside the same
# microseconds, and this invocation still refuses — but it is a window, not a
# guarantee. The alternative, a clobbering `mv` restore, would delete the third
# invocation's live lock instead, which is strictly worse.
#
# Returns 0 when this invocation displaced the victim AND now holds the lock,
# 2 when it displaced the victim but LOST the race to re-acquire (the caller
# must never displace twice), and 1 when no displacement was performed at all.
# 0 and 2 are both COMPLETED displacements — the victim's lock is gone in each —
# so the caller owes the loud warning on both, not only on the one that ends
# holding the lock.
_verify_lock_steal() {
  local lock="$1" victim="$2" target="$3" marker self taken moved rc=1
  self="$(_verify_lock_self_pid)"
  marker="$lock.prune"
  if ! { ln -s "$self" "$marker" 2>/dev/null && [ "$(readlink "$marker" 2>/dev/null)" = "$self" ]; }; then
    _verify_lock_recover_marker "$marker" "$(readlink "$marker" 2>/dev/null || true)" || true
    return 1
  fi
  VERIFY_PRUNE_MARKER="$marker"
  trap "$EXIT_TRAP" EXIT
  taken="$lock.stolen.$self"
  rm -rf "$taken" 2>/dev/null || true
  if mv "$lock" "$taken" 2>/dev/null; then
    moved="$(readlink "$taken" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    if [ "$moved" = "$victim" ]; then
      rm -rf "$taken" 2>/dev/null || true
      if _verify_lock_try_acquire "$lock" "$target"; then rc=0; else rc=2; fi
    else
      # MIS-TAKE: the entry we took is not the holder we waited on. Put it back
      # without clobbering anything that landed meanwhile, and refuse.
      if [ -n "$moved" ]; then
        ln -s "$moved" "$lock" 2>/dev/null || true
      fi
      rm -rf "$taken" 2>/dev/null || true
    fi
  fi
  rm -f "$marker" 2>/dev/null || true
  VERIFY_PRUNE_MARKER=""
  return "$rc"
}

# _verify_lock_prune_and_acquire <lockpath> <target>: prune a dead/garbage holder
# under the exclusive prune marker. Returns 0 only when this invocation now HOLDS
# the lock; returns 1 for every other outcome, and the caller then RESTARTS
# classification from the top rather than assuming the prune conferred anything.
_verify_lock_prune_and_acquire() {
  local lock="$1" target="$2" marker self mowner cls rc=1
  marker="$lock.prune"
  self="$(_verify_lock_self_pid)"
  if ! { ln -s "$self" "$marker" 2>/dev/null && [ "$(readlink "$marker" 2>/dev/null)" = "$self" ]; }; then
    # Another invocation is already pruning. Recover the marker if its owner
    # died holding it — EXCLUSIVELY, see _verify_lock_recover_marker — then back
    # off and restart classification from the top.
    mowner="$(readlink "$marker" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    _verify_lock_recover_marker "$marker" "$mowner" || true
    return 1
  fi
  VERIFY_PRUNE_MARKER="$marker"
  trap "$EXIT_TRAP" EXIT
  # Holding the marker, RE-READ the lock. Only a still-dead (or garbage) holder is
  # removed; a live holder that got there first is left alone and the caller falls
  # through to the normal live-holder path.
  cls="$(_verify_lock_classify "$lock")"
  case "$cls" in
    dead|garbage)
      rm -rf "$lock" 2>/dev/null || true
      if _verify_lock_try_acquire "$lock" "$target"; then rc=0; fi
      ;;
    free)
      if _verify_lock_try_acquire "$lock" "$target"; then rc=0; fi
      ;;
  esac
  rm -f "$marker" 2>/dev/null || true
  VERIFY_PRUNE_MARKER=""
  return "$rc"
}

# The one refusal both restart budgets print. It names the recovery-claim paths
# because the only way this is reached repeatedly is a recoverer SIGKILLed while
# holding one — the stated, operator-clearable residual of
# _verify_lock_recover_marker's refusal to break a claim it did not create.
VERIFY_LOCK_UNSETTLED_MSG='scripts/test: the verify lock never settled — refusing rather than proceeding under an uncertain lock state.
If this repeats, a pruner was killed holding a recovery claim: inspect and remove
<git-common-dir>/fishhawk-verify.lock.prune (or <FISHHAWK_VERIFY_LOCK_PATH>.prune when
the override is set) and its .reap.* siblings by hand.'

# _acquire_verify_lock_at <lockpath>: the acquisition loop. Returns 0 when this
# invocation may proceed (it holds the lock), 1 when it must REFUSE. Split from
# _acquire_verify_lock so the harness can drive every branch in-process against a
# fixture lock path with an injected self pid.
_acquire_verify_lock_at() {
  local lock="$1" kind target self cls holder budget poll steal_rc waited=0 announced=0 stole=0 restarts=0
  self="$(_verify_lock_self_pid)"
  kind="$(_verify_lock_kind)"
  target="$self:$kind"
  budget="$(_verify_lock_wait_budget)"
  poll="$(_verify_lock_poll_interval)"
  while :; do
    if _verify_lock_try_acquire "$lock" "$target"; then
      return 0
    fi
    cls="$(_verify_lock_classify "$lock")"
    holder="$(_verify_lock_holder_pid "$lock")"
    case "$cls" in
      free|dead|garbage)
        restarts=$((restarts + 1))
        if [ "$restarts" -gt 100 ]; then
          echo "$VERIFY_LOCK_UNSETTLED_MSG" >&2
          return 1
        fi
        if [ "$cls" = "free" ]; then
          continue
        fi
        if _verify_lock_prune_and_acquire "$lock" "$target"; then
          return 0
        fi
        continue
        ;;
      live-shell|live-runner)
        if [ "$kind" = "shell" ]; then
          cat >&2 <<EOF
scripts/test: another verify already holds the lock at $lock (pid ${holder:-unknown}, kind ${cls#live-}).
The runner owns verify for this stage. Do NOT poll or retry this in a loop — it is a
fast failure, not a queue. Run your package's tests directly instead:
  scripts/test single -run TestX ./your/package/...
EOF
          return 1
        fi
        if [ "$waited" -ge "$budget" ]; then
          if [ "$cls" = "live-runner" ]; then
            cat >&2 <<EOF
scripts/test: another RUNNER verify still holds the lock at $lock after ${budget}s
(holder pid ${holder:-unknown}, this pid $self). Two concurrent runner verifies on one
worktree family is a bug to surface, not one to paper over — refusing rather than
displacing a peer runner.
EOF
            return 1
          fi
          if [ "$stole" -eq 1 ]; then
            echo "scripts/test: the verify lock at $lock was retaken after a displacement — refusing rather than displacing twice" >&2
            return 1
          fi
          # Conditional displacement: the decision must still be VALID at the
          # unlink, not merely at the classification. _verify_lock_steal
          # completes only against this exact holder.
          steal_rc=0
          _verify_lock_steal "$lock" "$holder:shell" "$target" || steal_rc=$?
          # The warning is owed on EVERY COMPLETED displacement, not only on the
          # one that ended with this invocation holding the lock. rc=2 means the
          # victim's lock WAS removed and someone else won the freed path — the
          # deposed verify is running unprotected either way, which is exactly
          # the state the warning exists to announce. Gating the warning on rc=0
          # would let a real displacement pass silently.
          case "$steal_rc" in
            0|2)
              echo "scripts/test: WARNING: displacing a wedged shell verify holding $lock (deposed pid ${holder:-unknown}) after ${budget}s — two verifies may now contend on golangci-lint's global lock and the shared testcontainers Postgres." >&2
              ;;
          esac
          case "$steal_rc" in
            0)
              return 0
              ;;
            2)
              # The victim was displaced but another invocation won the free
              # lock. Never displace twice: the next expiry refuses.
              echo "scripts/test: the freed verify lock at $lock was taken by another invocation before this one could re-acquire it — this invocation will not displace again." >&2
              stole=1
              ;;
          esac
          restarts=$((restarts + 1))
          if [ "$restarts" -gt 100 ]; then
            echo "$VERIFY_LOCK_UNSETTLED_MSG" >&2
            return 1
          fi
          continue
        fi
        if [ "$announced" -eq 0 ]; then
          echo "scripts/test: waiting up to ${budget}s for the verify lock at $lock (holder pid ${holder:-unknown}, kind ${cls#live-})" >&2
          announced=1
        fi
        sleep "$poll"
        waited=$((waited + poll))
        continue
        ;;
    esac
  done
}

# _acquire_verify_lock: resolve the lock path and acquire. Returns 0 to proceed
# (held, or degraded to no locking) and 1 to refuse.
_acquire_verify_lock() {
  local lock
  if ! lock="$(_verify_lock_path)"; then
    return 0  # degrade: reason already printed, run without the lock
  fi
  _acquire_verify_lock_at "$lock"
}

# _release_verify_lock: EXIT-trap release. Removes the prune marker only when it
# still names us, and the lock only when it still names US — so a lock this
# invocation refused on, or one that was displaced out from under it, is never
# removed.
_release_verify_lock() {
  local self
  self="$(_verify_lock_self_pid)"
  if [ -n "${VERIFY_PRUNE_MARKER:-}" ] && [ "$(readlink "$VERIFY_PRUNE_MARKER" 2>/dev/null)" = "$self" ]; then
    rm -f "$VERIFY_PRUNE_MARKER" 2>/dev/null || true
  fi
  VERIFY_PRUNE_MARKER=""
  if [ -n "${VERIFY_LOCK_HELD:-}" ] && [ "$(readlink "$VERIFY_LOCK_HELD" 2>/dev/null)" = "${VERIFY_LOCK_TARGET:-}" ]; then
    rm -f "$VERIFY_LOCK_HELD" 2>/dev/null || true
  fi
  VERIFY_LOCK_HELD=""
  return 0
}

# verify: the runner's committed-tree implement gate. Lint FIRST so a fast
# format/lint failure leads the captured output and aborts (set -e) before the
# slow test loop. The doc line budget and schema-sync drift are checked NEXT
# (each <1s) so a docs defect fails in-loop, still ahead of the slow test loop,
# as are the gate harnesses, the site voice gate (#2261), the site IA gate
# (E12.7 / #2318), the ADR record gate (E78.1 / #3722), and the gate-image pin
# check (E51.17 / #3966). `--no-tests` stops right after those legs. The legs
# stay INLINE here rather than behind a helper: scripts/test-patch-coverage and
# scripts/test-schema-sync-verify assert their order by reading this function's
# own body.
# The AGGREGATE coverage gate is still intentionally omitted to bound in-loop
# runtime — it stays on `coverage` and in CI. What runs here instead is the
# PATCH-scoped gate (#1887), folded into the same test loop via -coverpkg so no
# second run happens. Every precondition degrades to a one-line reason plus the
# plain `cmd_test` loop; none of them can red-line verify.
cmd_verify() {
  if ! _acquire_verify_lock; then
    exit 1
  fi
  cmd_lint
  _verify_doc_line_budget
  _verify_schema_sync
  _verify_gate_harnesses
  _verify_site_voice
  _verify_site_ia
  _verify_adr_records
  _verify_gate_image_pins
  # --no-tests (E51.17 / #3966): the legs above need no Docker daemon, so they
  # are what the gate image can run. The test loop below needs testcontainers
  # Postgres, which the image path reaches only with full gate isolation.
  if [ "$VERIFY_NO_TESTS" = "1" ]; then
    echo "scripts/test: verify --no-tests — the go test loop and the patch-coverage gate are SKIPPED (the daemon-dependent tests need full gate isolation, #2137); the full verify still runs them" >&2
    return 0
  fi
  # SCOPED form (#3315). Bucketing fails SAFE: any package this layer cannot
  # place prints a reason and falls through to the full unscoped loop below.
  local buckets=""
  if [ -n "$VERIFY_PACKAGES" ] && buckets="$(_verify_scope_buckets "$VERIFY_PACKAGES")" && [ -n "$buckets" ]; then
    # The patch-coverage gate is SKIPPED, not narrowed. Its denominator is the
    # WHOLE branch diff, so a run that tested only a few packages cannot honestly
    # gate it — and narrowing the denominator to the scoped packages would be a
    # gate that reports a coverage verdict it did not earn. The #2124 pre-test
    # snapshot/digest machinery is therefore left completely UN-ARMED here rather
    # than armed and weakened; the full committed-tree gate still runs it.
    echo "scripts/test: scoped verify (--packages $VERIFY_PACKAGES) — the patch-coverage gate runs in the full committed-tree gate" >&2
    cmd_test_scoped "$buckets"
    return 0
  fi
  local base="" mods=""
  if [ -n "${FISHHAWK_SKIP_PATCH_COVERAGE:-}" ]; then
    echo "scripts/test: FISHHAWK_SKIP_PATCH_COVERAGE set — skipping the patch-coverage gate" >&2
  elif ! base="$(_patch_cov_base)"; then
    echo "scripts/test: no usable diff base (git absent, not a work tree, or unresolvable ref) — skipping the patch-coverage gate" >&2
  elif ! mods="$(_patch_cov_changed_modules "$base")"; then
    echo "scripts/test: could not bucket changed Go files (no merge base, or go/jq unavailable) — skipping the patch-coverage gate" >&2
  elif [ -z "$mods" ]; then
    echo "scripts/test: no changed Go packages vs $base — skipping the patch-coverage gate" >&2
  else
    cmd_test_with_patch_coverage "$mods" "$base"
    _verify_patch_coverage "$base"
    return 0
  fi
  cmd_test
}

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --in-gate-image (E51.17 / #3966)
#
# Runs `scripts/test lint` or `scripts/test verify --no-tests` inside the pinned
# gate image (deploy/gate-image) so a local run uses CI's exact tool versions.
# It is CI PARITY, NOT ISOLATION: the container has the network, the checkout is
# mounted read-write at its own absolute path, and it runs as the caller's
# uid:gid. Daemon-dependent tests stay out of reach until #2137.
#
# Host-side order, each step failing closed:
#   1. docker absent from PATH -> exit 1 naming docker. Never a skip. Argument
#      validation already ran in _parse_verify_args/_parse_lint_args.
#   2. verify only: take the HOST per-repository verify lock, refusing exactly
#      as `verify` does on a live holder. Lock liveness is a `kill -0` PID
#      check, which cannot cross a PID namespace, so a container sharing the
#      host lock would judge a live host holder dead and displace it. The host
#      lock is therefore the real one, and the container gets a private
#      FISHHAWK_VERIFY_LOCK_PATH. lint stays lock-free, as on the host.
#   3. resolve $FISHHAWK_TEST_GATE_IMAGE (default GATE_IMAGE_DEFAULT) to an
#      image ID, pulling it once when it is not present locally.
#   4. run scripts/check-gate-image --image <ID> (approval condition 1): every
#      tool version in the image must equal this checkout's Dockerfile pins, so
#      a stale locally cached :main refuses instead of producing results that
#      look CI-identical. The inner command then runs on that same ID, never on
#      the tag, so the image checked is the image run.
#   5. read the image's /etc/passwd and, when no entry maps the caller's uid,
#      append one (the host login name, home /tmp) and mount the result
#      read-only over /etc/passwd. --user uid:gid alone leaves the uid
#      nameless, so whoami fails and test-helm-render r9h's `$(whoami)` probe
#      degrades to an empty needle that matches every argv (#3966 acceptance).
#   6. docker run the inner command and exit with its status.
#
# The variable is FISHHAWK_TEST_GATE_IMAGE, deliberately NOT FISHHAWK_GATE_IMAGE:
# exporting the latter in a shell that spawns the runner switches its gate onto
# the container path, where this repository's verify fails until #2137.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

GATE_IMAGE_DEFAULT="ghcr.io/kuhlman-labs/fishhawk-gate:main"

# _gate_image_resolve_id <ref>: print the image ID (`sha256:...`) of <ref>,
# pulling it once when `docker image inspect` cannot see it locally. Returns
# non-zero when neither yields an ID.
_gate_image_resolve_id() {
  local ref="$1" out
  if ! out="$(docker image inspect --format '{{.Id}}' "$ref" 2>/dev/null)"; then
    echo "scripts/test: gate image $ref is not present locally — pulling it" >&2
    if ! docker pull "$ref" >&2; then
      return 1
    fi
    if ! out="$(docker image inspect --format '{{.Id}}' "$ref" 2>/dev/null)"; then
      return 1
    fi
  fi
  out="${out%%[[:space:]]*}"
  case "$out" in
    sha256:?*) printf '%s' "$out" ;;
    *) return 1 ;;
  esac
}

# _gate_image_common_dir: print the git common dir when it lies OUTSIDE $ROOT
# (a linked worktree), so it can be mounted at its own path; print nothing for a
# primary checkout, a non-repo, or a host without git. A linked worktree's .git
# FILE names the common dir by absolute path, so without this mount every git
# command inside the container fails.
_gate_image_common_dir() {
  local common root_p common_p
  command -v git >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  common="$(git -C "$ROOT" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null)" || return 0
  if [ -z "$common" ] || [ ! -d "$common" ]; then
    return 0
  fi
  root_p="$(cd "$ROOT" && pwd -P)"
  common_p="$(cd "$common" && pwd -P)"
  case "$common_p/" in
    "$root_p"/*) return 0 ;;
  esac
  printf '%s' "$common"
}

# _gate_image_passwd <id> <file>: write <file> as image <id>'s /etc/passwd plus,
# when no entry there maps the caller's uid, one that does. The name is the host
# login name, or fishhawk-gate when that is empty or not a plain
# [A-Za-z0-9._-] word (a `:` or newline would corrupt the file). Returns
# non-zero when the image's /etc/passwd cannot be read.
_gate_image_passwd() {
  local id="$1" file="$2" uid gid name
  uid="$(id -u)"
  gid="$(id -g)"
  if ! docker run --rm --entrypoint '' "$id" cat /etc/passwd > "$file" 2>/dev/null; then
    return 1
  fi
  if awk -F: -v u="$uid" '$3 == u { found = 1 } END { exit !found }' "$file"; then
    return 0
  fi
  name="$(id -un 2>/dev/null || true)"
  case "$name" in
    "" | -* | *[!A-Za-z0-9._-]*) name="fishhawk-gate" ;;
  esac
  printf '%s:x:%s:%s:fishhawk gate caller:/tmp:/bin/sh\n' "$name" "$uid" "$gid" >> "$file"
}

# cmd_in_gate_image <lint|verify>: see the block comment above.
cmd_in_gate_image() {
  local mode="$1" image id cache common pwdir rc=0
  local inner=() args=()
  if ! command -v docker >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    cat >&2 <<'EOF'
scripts/test: docker not found on PATH — --in-gate-image runs the gate inside the pinned gate image and needs docker (fail closed, not a skip).
Start or install Docker, or run the gate on the host without --in-gate-image.
EOF
    exit 1
  fi
  if [ "$mode" = "verify" ]; then
    if ! _acquire_verify_lock; then
      exit 1
    fi
    inner=(scripts/test verify --no-tests)
  else
    inner=(scripts/test lint)
  fi
  image="${FISHHAWK_TEST_GATE_IMAGE:-$GATE_IMAGE_DEFAULT}"
  if ! id="$(_gate_image_resolve_id "$image")"; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF
scripts/test: cannot resolve gate image $image to an image ID (docker image inspect and docker pull both failed) — fail closed.
Build one locally and point the helper at it:
  docker buildx build --load -t fishhawk-gate:local deploy/gate-image
  FISHHAWK_TEST_GATE_IMAGE=fishhawk-gate:local scripts/test $mode --in-gate-image
EOF
    exit 1
  fi
  echo "scripts/test: gate image $image resolved to $id" >&2
  if [ ! -x "$ROOT/scripts/check-gate-image" ]; then
    echo "scripts/test: scripts/check-gate-image missing or not executable — cannot check the gate image's pins, refusing to run (fail closed)" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  if ! "$ROOT/scripts/check-gate-image" --root "$ROOT" --image "$id" >&2; then
    echo "scripts/test: gate image $image ($id) does not match this checkout's pins — refusing to run, because a stale image cannot produce CI-identical results. Pull a fresh one (docker pull $image) or rebuild it (deploy/gate-image/README.md)." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  cache="${FISHHAWK_GATE_CACHE_DIR:-${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-${HOME:-/tmp}/.cache}/fishhawk-gate}"
  if ! mkdir -p "$cache/gocache" "$cache/gomodcache" "$cache/lintcache" 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "scripts/test: cannot create the gate-image cache dirs under $cache — set FISHHAWK_GATE_CACHE_DIR to a writable directory" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  # Under the cache dir, not $TMPDIR: it must be a path the docker daemon can
  # bind-mount, which the cache dir already has to be.
  if ! pwdir="$(mktemp -d "$cache/passwd.XXXXXX")"; then
    echo "scripts/test: cannot create a passwd scratch dir under $cache — set FISHHAWK_GATE_CACHE_DIR to a writable directory" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  if ! _gate_image_passwd "$id" "$pwdir/passwd"; then
    rm -rf "$pwdir"
    echo "scripts/test: cannot read /etc/passwd from gate image $id to give uid $(id -u) a name inside it — refusing to run (fail closed)" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  args=(run --rm --entrypoint '' --user "$(id -u):$(id -g)" -v "$pwdir/passwd:/etc/passwd:ro" -v "$ROOT:$ROOT" -w "$ROOT")
  common="$(_gate_image_common_dir)"
  if [ -n "$common" ]; then
    args+=(-v "$common:$common")
  fi
  # Never a docker socket: the image carries no docker CLI either.
  # GIT_CONFIG_COUNT/KEY/VALUE is COMMAND-scope config, so it survives the
  # GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL/SYSTEM=/dev/null pins the inner scripts/test exports, for
  # a bind mount whose ownership the container may see as foreign.
  args+=(
    -v "$cache/gocache:/gocache"
    -v "$cache/gomodcache:/gomodcache"
    -v "$cache/lintcache:/lintcache"
    -e HOME=/tmp
    -e GOPATH=/tmp/gopath
    -e GOCACHE=/gocache
    -e GOMODCACHE=/gomodcache
    -e GOLANGCI_LINT_CACHE=/lintcache
    -e GOTOOLCHAIN=local
    -e FISHHAWK_VERIFY_LOCK_PATH=/tmp/fishhawk-verify.lock
    -e GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1
    -e GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=safe.directory
    -e GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0='*'
    "$id" "${inner[@]}"
  )
  echo "scripts/test: running '${inner[*]}' in $id (network on, checkout mounted read-write: CI parity, not isolation)" >&2
  docker "${args[@]}" || rc=$?
  rm -rf "$pwdir"
  exit "$rc"
}

cmd_single() {
  if [ "$#" -eq 0 ]; then
    cat >&2 <<'EOF'
usage: scripts/test single <args>
example: scripts/test single -run TestNotifyPlanReady ./backend/internal/issuecomment/...
EOF
    exit 1
  fi
  go test -race "$@"
}

usage() {
  cat <<'EOF'
usage: scripts/test [SUBCOMMAND] [ARGS]

  test                 (default) run `go test -race ./...` in every module; every
                       module runs, then a summary names each failed module
  coverage             run tests + aggregate coverage gate
  lint                 run `golangci-lint run --allow-serial-runners ./...` in every module
  verify               lint + schema-sync + tests + patch-scoped coverage — the runner's verify gate
  verify --packages P  the SCOPED verify form: P is a comma-separated list of
                       repo-relative package DIRECTORIES (e.g.
                       backend/internal/run,cli/internal/spec). Every non-test
                       leg is unchanged; the `go test -race` loop is restricted
                       to those packages and the patch-coverage gate is skipped.
                       Falls back to $FISHHAWK_VERIFY_PACKAGES when absent.
  verify --no-tests    every verify leg except the test loop and the
                       patch-coverage gate (rejected with --packages)
  lint --in-gate-image
  verify --in-gate-image
                       run `lint` / `verify --no-tests` inside the pinned gate
                       image for CI parity; the image's pins are checked first
                       and a mismatch refuses. Needs docker. Image:
                       $FISHHAWK_TEST_GATE_IMAGE, default
                       ghcr.io/kuhlman-labs/fishhawk-gate:main
  single ARGS          pass-through to `go test -race ARGS`
  -h | --help | help   show this message
EOF
}

# Bottom dispatch is skipped when sourced lib-only (FISHHAWK_TEST_LIB_ONLY=1),
# so scripts/test-container-lease can exercise the lease helpers without running
# a subcommand. Behavior is unchanged when the var is unset.
if [ -z "${FISHHAWK_TEST_LIB_ONLY:-}" ]; then
  case "${1:-test}" in
    test|"")          cmd_test ;;
    coverage|cover)   cmd_coverage ;;
    lint)
      shift; _parse_lint_args "$@"
      if [ "$GATE_IMAGE_MODE" = "1" ]; then cmd_in_gate_image lint; else cmd_lint; fi
      ;;
    verify)
      shift; _parse_verify_args "$@"
      if [ "$GATE_IMAGE_MODE" = "1" ]; then cmd_in_gate_image verify; else cmd_verify; fi
      ;;
    single)           shift; cmd_single "$@" ;;
    -h|--help|help)   usage ;;
    *)
      echo "scripts/test: unknown subcommand: $1" >&2
      usage >&2
      exit 1
      ;;
  esac
fi
